  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Massachusetts received $16.8B in contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 93,724 awards and the average award size was $179K. Obligations are down 13.0% year over year, and current-year totals are partial because the fiscal year is still in progress. Fiscal year obligations have ranged from $24.2B in FY21 to $16.8B in FY26, with intermediate totals of $17.9B in FY22, $18.3B in FY23, $17.8B in FY24, and $19.3B in FY25. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $13B in the current fiscal year to date. Commercial prime vendors in the state include large defense and research organizations. RAYTHEON COMPANY leads vendor receipts with $3.4B, followed by GENERAL ELECTRIC COMPANY at $1.3B and MASSACHUSETTS INSTITUTE OF TECHNOLOGY at $1.2B. Manufacturing and technical services sectors collect the largest industry shares, led by Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $3.3B and Engineering Services at $1.8B. The data reflect a concentrated defense presence alongside a strong research and engineering base.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting trended in Massachusetts?",
        "body": "Massachusetts obligations were $24.2B in FY21 and are $16.8B in FY26. Obligations moved through $17.9B in FY22, $18.3B in FY23, $17.8B in FY24, and $19.3B in FY25. Obligations are down 13.0% year over year in the current fiscal period."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries dominate contracting in Massachusetts?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $13B in obligations in FY2026 to date. The next largest buyers are the Department of Veterans Affairs at $927M, the Department of Transportation at $754M, and the Department of Health and Human Services at $616M. Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing is the largest industry at $3.3B, followed by Engineering Services at $1.8B and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Biotechnology) at $1.7B."
      }
